CHAPTER 11
Data Persistence: UserDefaults, Core Data, and
SwiftData
Like Android, iOS offers persistence mechanisms suited to different data scales, from a single boolean flag up to a fully
relational offline dataset.
UserDefaults
For small key-value settings — a theme choice, an onboarding-completed flag — UserDefaults is the simplest option,
backed by a property-list file and cached in memory for fast synchronous access.
UserDefaults.standard.set(true, forKey: "darkModeEnabled")
let isDarkMode = UserDefaults.standard.bool(forKey: "darkModeEnabled")
Core Data
Core Data is Apple's long-standing object-graph and persistence framework, built on SQLite. Data models are defined
visually in a .xcdatamodeld file as entities with attributes and relationships, and Xcode generates matching
NSManagedObject subclasses automatically.
let context = persistentContainer.viewContext
let user = UserEntity(context: context)
user.name = "Susi"
try? context.save()
let request = UserEntity.fetchRequest()
let allUsers = try? context.fetch(request)
SwiftData
SwiftData, introduced in iOS 17, is Apple's modern replacement for Core Data, using plain Swift classes annotated with
@Model instead of a separate visual schema editor, and integrating directly with SwiftUI through the @Query property
wrapper.
@Model
class UserModel {
var name: String
var email: String
init(name: String, email: String) {
self.name = name; self.email = email
}

retrieved_texts.ios-development-documentation-f1c885:p9::
App States

Not running — the app has not been launched, or was terminated by the system.

Inactive — the app is in the foreground but not receiving events (for example, during a phone call interruption).

Active — the app is in the foreground and receiving events normally.

Background — the app is executing code but not visible on screen; background time is limited and must be requested
explicitly for longer tasks.

Suspended — the app is in memory but not executing any code; it may be purged from memory at any time under
system pressure.
SwiftUI Scene Phase
struct MyAppApp: App {
@Environment(\.scenePhase) private var scenePhase
var body: some Scene {
WindowGroup { ContentView() }
.onChange(of: scenePhase) { _, newPhase in
switch newPhase {
case .active: print("App became active")
case .inactive: print("App became inactive")
case .background: print("App entered background")
@unknown default: break
}
}
}
}
UIKit AppDelegate and SceneDelegate
In UIKit apps, app-wide events (launch, background, termination, push notification registration) are handled in the
AppDelegate, while per-window UI lifecycle events (a scene connecting, disconnecting, or resigning active status) are
handled in a SceneDelegate — a split introduced to properly support multiple simultaneous windows on iPad.
func applicationDidEnterBackground(_ application: UIApplication) {
saveUserProgress()
}
func applicationWillEnterForeground(_ application: UIApplication) {
refreshIfNeeded()
}
Practical takeaway
Persist important user data as soon as it changes rather than waiting for a background callback, since a
suspended app can be terminated by the system with no further notification.
CHAPTER 8
Auto Layout and Constraints
iOS devices ship in a wide range of screen sizes, and a single layout must adapt correctly to all of them, as well as to
rotation, Split View on iPad, and Dynamic Type text scaling. Auto Layout is Apple's constraint-based system for
describing view positions and sizes relative to one another rather than with fixed pixel coordinates.
Auto Layout in UIKit
let button = UIButton(type: .system)
button.translatesAutoresizingMaskIntoConstraints = false
view.addSubview(button)

