case .active: print("App became active")
case .inactive: print("App became inactive")
case .background: print("App entered background")
@unknown default: break
}
}
}
}
UIKit AppDelegate and SceneDelegate
In UIKit apps, app-wide events (launch, background, termination, push notification registration) are handled in the
AppDelegate, while per-window UI lifecycle events (a scene connecting, disconnecting, or resigning active status) are
handled in a SceneDelegate — a split introduced to properly support multiple simultaneous windows on iPad.
func applicationDidEnterBackground(_ application: UIApplication) {
saveUserProgress()
}
func applicationWillEnterForeground(_ application: UIApplication) {
refreshIfNeeded()
}
Practical takeaway
Persist important user data as soon as it changes rather than waiting for a background callback, since a
suspended app can be terminated by the system with no further notification.
